# MoE host CPU compute tier: decode-path redirect and kernel sentinel skip
#
# M14 (0.3.0). Companion to patches/0011 (the CPU kernel itself, no runtime
# wiring - MOE_CPU_TIER exists as a property and does nothing with only 0011
# applied). This patch is the wiring: which (token,expert) pairs get redirected
# to the host tier (LRU probe, capacity-miss policy), how the GEMV kernels are
# told to skip them, and how the host excursion overlaps the GPU work and
# joins before mlp_reduce.
#
# Correction to the predecessor design (docs/design-m9-offload-v2.md §2) and
# to the task brief: the OTD weight tensors are NOT host-addressable today.
# fill_weights_memory (moe_otd_runtime.cpp) reads each tensor with
# ParallelWeightReader::read() -> positional_read() on a plain file handle,
# into a loop-local std::vector<uint8_t> payload; there is no mmap and no
# usm_host mirror of the expert weights (the usm_host buffers that exist are
# the staging ring and the device/host-tier slot buffers, both destinations,
# never sources). Reusing payload for the CPU tier would cost a 1.6 MiB pread
# plus memcpy per expert per token on the decode critical path. Patch 0011
# adds ParallelWeightReader::mapped(offset, size): a lazily-created, read-only
# mmap of the same weight file, so a run that never enables the tier never
# creates the mapping, and the CPU tier reads the exact bytes the GPU upload
# path would have read, at the cost of a page-cache reference instead of a
# copy.
#
# Weight nibble order (the fact patch 0011's kernel and this patch's redirect
# both depend on, reproduced here because a reader who only looks at the
# GEMV's lane pairing will conclude the wrong thing). For a 4-bit weight
# column, byte j holds element 2j in the low nibble and element 2j+1 in the
# high nibble - standard packing. The GEMV's lane pairing
# (moe_3gemm_swiglu_mlp.cl, the ELEMS_PER_LANE inner loops, e.g. the ==4
# branch) *looks* half-block interleaved, but that is a staging-buffer
# artifact, not the stored layout: the x2 SLM staging de-interleaves x first
# (for each FAKE_GROUP block, px2[j] = px[2j] and
# px2[j + FAKE_GROUP_SIZE/2] = px[2j+1]). Substituting that staging map back
# through lane L's read (a.s0 = x2[gk*FG + L], a.s2 = x2[gk*FG + FG/2 + L],
# paired with DEQUANT_4BIT_LO(b.s0) / DEQUANT_4BIT_HI(b.s0)) yields LO -> x[gk*FG
# + 2L], HI -> x[gk*FG + 2L + 1] - i.e. the ordinary 2j/2j+1 packing, with the
# apparent interleave coming entirely from x2, not from the weight bytes. The
# same substitution holds in the ELEMS_PER_LANE==2 and ==8 branches. Scale/zp
# axes: file layout is the transpose of the device layout
# (maybe_transpose_scale_zp maps src[o*group_count+g] -> dst[g*oc+o] on
# upload), so the CPU reads scale/zp as [oc][group] and must NOT transpose -
# doing so produces an O(1) error whenever oc != group_count, true for every
# served shape, which is why patches/0011's T1 red case 2 exists.
#
# Policy decision (§2.4 of the binding spec; do not re-open without new
# measurement). Chosen: a capacity miss - one that would evict a resident
# expert from the device LRU pool - is redirected to the CPU tier with no LRU
# insertion and no async promotion. A miss taken while the pool still has a
# never-claimed free slot still uploads (warm-up, identical to the pre-M14
# path); once the pool is full, the partition between "first-touch resident"
# and "CPU tier" is static for the rest of the run.
#
#   Rejected A - insert without filling: a later probe() would report the
#   expert resident and hand the GEMV an unfilled slot. Silent garbage, not a
#   correctness bug you can find by inspection.
#
#   Rejected B - insert and upload asynchronously off the critical path: pays
#   the exact PCIe cost M14 exists to remove, and pays it for the cold tail
#   (the set the LRU has already ranked below everything resident). Under a
#   decode stream that keeps missing, async uploads queue behind each other on
#   the in-order queue and re-enter the critical path through the staging
#   ring's acquire() wait; it also re-introduces evictions of the hot set that
#   patch 0004's counters were built to show were absent.
#
#   Rejected C - frequency-based admission with promotion after K hits: the
#   right answer if the closing measurement shows the first-touch resident set
#   is a poor approximation of the hot set. Not the right answer to guess at -
#   nothing measured says the hot set drifts. Parked; the gpu_hit_rate T4
#   reports is the number that would argue for it.
#
# Kernel skip mechanism, five-point correctness argument (quote-verified
# against the tree this patch applies to):
#   1. scratch.up rows of a skipped pair are left stale by mlp_gate_up.
#      mlp_down skips exactly the same pairs (same expert_list, same flat_id
#      decomposition in both kernels), so no stale scratch.up value is ever
#      read - no NaN can propagate from an unwritten row.
#   2. scratch.y rows of a skipped pair are left stale by mlp_down and are
#      unconditionally overwritten by the host DMA in cpu_tier_join(), enqueued
#      before mlp_reduce.
#   3. The shared expert (expert_slot == MAX_TOPK) never takes the sentinel
#      branch: it has no LRU slot and cannot be a capacity miss.
#   4. Barrier legality: local size in dimension 0 is 1 for both the gate_up
#      and down stages (exec_batched_gemv's execute_stage calls,
#      moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt.cpp - the workgroup dispatch that predates this
#      patch and that this patch does not change), so flat_id and expert_id
#      are uniform across every work-item of a workgroup. A workgroup that
#      returns before its barrier does so as a whole; no work-item of it ever
#      reaches the barrier. This is the same uniformity the pre-existing
#      dispatch already relies on (one workgroup, one (token,expert) pair).
#   5. With MOE_CPU_TIER off, MOE_CPU_TIER_ENABLE is not jitted
#      (add_common_consts checks config.get_moe_cpu_tier() and only defines it
#      when true), the preprocessor removes both #ifdef MOE_CPU_TIER_ENABLE
#      blocks in moe_3gemm_swiglu_mlp.cl entirely, and the kernel source fed to
#      the compiler is byte-for-byte the pre-patch source. Not independently
#      verified by diffing compiled binaries (no card in this environment);
#      verification is the preprocessor argument above plus grep of the
#      generated kernel sources directory in the build log for the sentinel
#      symbol, owed to the T4 window.
#
# Event ordering (why cpu_tier_join's placement between `down` and `reduce` is
# sufficient, not merely convenient). Correctness needs exactly one thing: the
# host DMA must land after `down` and before `reduce`. Both hold by program
# order on the in-order queue (execution_config.cpp forces in_order whenever
# use_onednn is set, true for both cards this milestone targets) - the same
# guarantee patches 0006/0007 already lean on. It does not need to land after
# gate_up/down for *conflict* reasons: with the sentinel skip those kernels
# never write the miss rows, so there is no write-write race to order against;
# the requirement is purely read-after-write against mlp_reduce, which reads
# every EXPERTS_PER_TOKEN row unconditionally (untouched by this patch on
# purpose - the host, not the kernel, supplies the missing ones).
#
# The CPU is not bit-equal to the GPU, and that is intentional (rounding,
# patches/0011 §5.4, restated here because it is the source of G1b's
# tolerance, not a defect this patch owes a fix for): the GPU accumulates
# within-fake-group partial products in `half` before scaling to float
# (moe_3gemm_swiglu_mlp.cl's ELEMS_PER_LANE inner loops); the CPU kernel
# accumulates in f32 throughout and rounds to f16 only at the three stage
# boundaries the GPU itself stores at (up/gate SwiGLU intermediate, down
# output). Patches/0011's T1c predicts the resulting divergence host-only, by
# emulating the GPU's half accumulation at (SUBGROUP_SIZE, ELEMS_PER_LANE) for
# both served cards; T4 compares the measured max-abs logit diff against that
# prediction. A measured diff far above the prediction is a layout bug this
# patch's kernel skip or the redirect above got wrong, not float noise -
# not this patch's job to have found, since T4 is on-card and this
# environment has no card.
#
# Deviation from the binding spec, stated rather than hidden: §5.6's two-phase
# per-expert column split (gate/up+SwiGLU as one wave, down as a second,
# CHUNK-sized wave, so a single expert's down-stage columns can be spread
# across pool threads without recomputing gate/up) is not implemented.
# moe_cpu_expert.hpp's public API (patches/0011) exposes only whole-pipeline
# calls (moe_cpu_expert[_ref|_avx2](desc, jobs, n_jobs, col_begin, col_end) -
# gate/up/SwiGLU computed in full for every call, only the down stage's
# columns are split) with no separate entry point for the intermediate `h`
# buffer between gate/up and down, so intra-expert splitting is not reachable
# from this file without changing that header, which is patches/0011's file.
# dispatch_cpu_tier() instead issues one CpuTierPool task per missed expert
# (col_begin=0, col_end=hidden), parallelising across experts rather than
# within one. Correct, and sufficient parallelism at the served topk (up to 8
# missing experts per token); a token with very few misses gets less thread
# utilisation than the spec's design would have given it. Left as a follow-up,
# not implemented here, because closing it means extending patches/0011's
# public header, which belongs to that patch.
#
# Fusion-impact obligation (0.3.0 ground rule 2): OWED to the T4 window, not
# discharged here. This patch changes a jit constant, a host-side branch in
# on_before_batched_gemv/exec_batched_gemv, and two early-return lines in the
# .cl source; it does not touch the fusion matcher, the primitive's declared
# inputs, or any constant's allocation type, so the expectation is that the
# base model still fuses x40 with the tier on - but that expectation is
# unverified without a compiled-graph dump on real hardware, which this
# environment cannot produce. T4 Step "Fusion-impact obligation" must record
# that dump before this obligation is considered discharged.
#
# Ground rule 3's survey obligation (ik_llama.cpp -ncmoe/--cpu-moe,
# llama.cpp's hybrid offload on comparable silicon) is likewise not
# discharged by this patch; M14 does not close until that survey is recorded
# alongside T4's numbers.
#
# T2 (tests/unit/test_cases/moe_3gemm_gpu_test.cpp,
# moe_3gemm_cpu_tier.sentinel_skip_leaves_rows_untouched), red-first, device-
# required, NOT run in this environment (no GPU device here; the orchestrator
# runs it in the end window) - see that test's comment block for the full
# argument. Summary: it isolates the kernel sentinel skip from both the
# redirect policy (§2, untested by T2 - a RESIDENT provider is used, so
# OffloadExpertWeightProvider::set_cpu_tier() is never called) and the CPU
# compute tier (§4/§5 - _cpu_tier stays false for a non-offloaded provider, so
# dispatch_cpu_tier/cpu_tier_join never run). MOE_CPU_TIER_ENABLE still gets
# jitted, because add_common_consts reads the raw property off the program
# config rather than the impl's _cpu_tier flag. TOPK_INDICES is fed as a plain
# input_layout (not routed through MoERouterFused), so the sentinel value
# (0xFFFFFFFF, read back as int -1) can be placed at an arbitrary flat
# position without an LRU pool. scratch.y has no public pre-fill hook, so the
# test runs the same network twice on the same hidden_states/topk_weights,
# changing only topk_indices, and diffs get_intermediates_memories()'s
# MOE_INTERNAL_BUFFER_DOWN_OUTPUT slot (index 1, moe_3gemm_base.hpp, mirrored
# as a literal since that header is off this test target's include path) byte
# for byte between the two runs. Red argument (cannot be executed here, stated
# for the reviewer instead): without the `if (expert_id ==
# MOE_CPU_TIER_SENTINEL) return;` lines, expert_id == -1 at the sentineled
# positions is used as `gate_weight_addr + (-1) * expert_wei_size` - an
# out-of-bounds __global pointer dereferenced by every lane of the workgroup -
# which on the target hardware is a GPU page fault or kernel-launch failure,
# not silently-wrong-but-passing data; the binding spec accepts a crash as a
# valid red outcome for exactly this reason.
#
# Integration notes (orchestrator, after the implementer's hand-off):
#  - Three of the pre-registered counters (cpu_compute_ns, cpu_x_readback_ns,
#    cpu_y_writeback_ns) were declared and reported but never incremented.
#    Wired: compute = wall time from dispatch() to the end of wait() (so it
#    includes whatever the GPU-side `down` stage left the join to hide);
#    x = the two blocking copy_to() readbacks; y = the host-side memcpy into
#    the staging ring plus the enqueue of the device writeback. All measured
#    with steady_clock on the thread that runs the primitive.
#  - The one-line "cpu_tier on:" configuration report used GPU_DEBUG_COUT,
#    dead in a non-debug-caps build (see 0011); it now goes to std::cerr,
#    gated on the MOE_OTD_PERF_LOG opt-in like the counter dump.
#  - moe_3gemm_swiglu_opt_impl::clone() uses make_deep_copy, which default-
#    constructs the clone and copies an explicit field list, and the clone
#    is the impl the network runs. Without the M14 members in that list the
#    tier was configured on the program's impl and dormant on the executing
#    one. Measured before the fix: tier ON gave cpu_tier_pairs=0 against
#    evictions=20294 and acquisitions identical to tier OFF, which the
#    probe()+get_lru_item() double count rules out for a tier branch that
#    ran even once. The members are now copied in clone(), with a fresh
#    StagingRing per clone (its entries track in-flight copies on this
#    impl's stream and cannot be shared).
#  - The x and routing-weight readbacks are enqueued non-blocking and joined
#    with one wait_for_events. Measured avg_cpu_x_us 350 -> 283 per layer;
#    the remaining cost is the queue round trip of the first readback, not
#    the bytes (x is token_num*hidden*2, the routing weights a few dozen).
#  - The task lambda in dispatch_cpu_tier() stamps the two worker-side
#    counters declared in 0011 (task lag, task duration).
#  - T2 red was run on a card at the end of the closing window, not only
#    argued: with both `if (expert_id == MOE_CPU_TIER_SENTINEL) return;`
#    lines removed from the kernel, sentinel_skip_leaves_rows_untouched
#    FAILED; restored, it passes, and the rebuilt plugin is byte-identical
#    to the one the window measured.
#
# Closing notes (orchestrator, after the measurement windows; DESIGN §7.0.2x
# is the durable record):
#  - "T4 compares the measured max-abs logit diff against that prediction"
#    above could not be done: the engine's endpoint exposes no logprobs.
#    The gate ran as byte-identity of 64 greedy tokens OFF vs ON at two
#    cells (ratio 50 / 8 GiB and ratio 75 / 5 GiB) plus the acceptance task
#    at 10/10 with the tier on. T1c's prediction stands recorded, uncompared.
#  - The compiled-graph dump the fusion-impact obligation asked for is not
#    producible on a plugin built without DEBUG_CAPS (the dump path is
#    inert; 0 files). Discharged by construction instead: the "cpu_tier on:"
#    line prints once per moe_3gemm_fused_compressed impl at program build,
#    and both tier-ON logs carry exactly forty of them. An unfused MoE would
#    print none.
#  - Ground rule 3's llama.cpp / ik_llama.cpp hybrid-offload survey was
#    dropped by the user's decision that CPU llama.cpp is not M14's bar
#    (docs/milestone-0.3.0.md, M14 row); it is not recorded and not owed.
#  - Point 5's grep of the generated kernel sources for the sentinel symbol
#    with the property off was NOT run; still owed. What was measured: with
#    the property off the host side never dispatched (cpu_tier_pairs=0 with
#    no "cpu_tier on" line) and the text is byte-identical to the tier-ON
#    text, which does not by itself prove the jit define absent.
#  - The T2 red outcome was measured, not only argued: with both sentinel
#    early-returns removed, sentinel_skip_leaves_rows_untouched FAILED on an
#    assertion (the rows differed) - the out-of-bounds read did NOT fault on
#    the test's card. So the red case is a wrong-data case there, and the
#    "GPU page fault" prediction above is retracted per §7.0.1; the test
#    catches it either way.
#  - The 8-bit weight path, f16/f32 zero points and non-f16 scales are now
#    refused at construction (found in review: the 8-bit scalar path has no
